-              <p>On \(S_\epsilon\), \(f\) is bounded and continuous, so its integral exists. The swap \(\sigma(x,y) = (y,x)\) maps \(S_\epsilon\) onto itself, and \(f(\sigma(x,y)) = -f(x,y)\). The integral is invariant under this measure-preserving map, a standard fact used without proof (listed in §9), which gives \(\int_{S_\epsilon} f = \int_{S_\epsilon} f\circ\sigma = -\int_{S_\epsilon} f\), so \(\int_{S_\epsilon} f = 0\). The value \(0\) is the limit of the truncated integrals, but Proposition 3.2 shows that \(\int |f| = \infty\) on the full square. A limit of integrals over truncations does not make \(f\) integrable, because the integrability in Assumption F is of \(|f|\) on the whole space.</p>
